      "text": "We start by solving the first equation (ln(x/2)-3)=(5/3), which yields (x = 1/2*exp(-4/3). We then calculate (y = -4/3*exp(-4/3)). After that, the coordinates in spherical coordinates are determined by (rho = sqrt(x^2 + y^2 + 1)), (theta = arctan2(y, x)), and (phi = arccos frac{-sqrt(2)}{sqrt{2}}).\nThe symmetry arguments still apply for the azimuthal angle (theta), ensuring that (theta) remains within the range [0, 2pi). For the polar angle (phi), we simplify to (phi = 2*arccos frac{y}{rho}). \n\nSo the spherical coordinates are ((rho, theta, phi)) which in Python code can be represented as:\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef calculate_sphere_coordinates(x, y, z):\n    \"\"\"\n    This function calculates the spherical coordinates of a point in 3-dimensional space.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    x (float): The x-coordinate of the point.\n    y (float): The y-coordinate of the point.\n    z (float): The z-coordinate of the point.\n    \n    Returns:\n    tuple: The spherical coordinates of the point.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    # Calculate the rho (distance from the origin)\n    rho = math.sqrt(x**2 + y**2 + z**2)\n    \n    # Calculate the theta (angle from the positive x-axis)\n    theta = math.atan2(y, x)\n    \n    # Calculate the phi (angle from the positive z-axis)\n    phi = math.acos(z / rho)\n    \n    return (rho, theta, phi)\n```\n\nTo summarize:\n- The spherical coordinate (rho = sqrt(x^2 + y^2 + 1)) arises from transforming the distance in the Euclidean space.\n- (theta = arctan2(y, x)) gives the direction, accounting for the different quadrants.\n- (phi = arccos frac{-sqrt(2)}{sqrt{2}}) maps the negative z-coordinate to 0 because we physically orient the coordinate system such that (phi = 0) corresponds to the positive z-axis.\n \nThus, the spherical coordinates are ( ((rho, theta, phi)) = (text{(rho, arctan2(y, x), arccos(-sqrt(2)/sqrt(2))}})).",
